9.5  The VFS: one interface for every filesystem

TL;DR
  - Programs call open, read and write the same way whatever holds the file. The
    kernel's VFS turns those calls into the right filesystem's code.
  - So the same cat reads ext4 on a disk, NFS on a server, /proc made up by the
    kernel, or a FUSE filesystem written in Python.
  - FUSE runs a filesystem as an ordinary program: sshfs, rclone, ntfs-3g,
    AppImages.

      cat /proc/filesystems    # filesystems this kernel knows
      findmnt -t nfs,nfs4,cifs,fuse.sshfs,virtiofs

On your machine
  /         btrfs on /dev/nvme0n1p2 · SSD mode
  /home     btrfs on /dev/nvme0n1p2 · SSD mode
  /var/lib/my data btrfs on /dev/nvme0n1p2 · SSD mode
  /boot/efi vfat on /dev/nvme0n1p1
  /media/usb exfat on /dev/sda
  /home     btrfs on /dev/nvme0n1p2

  Kernel    6.12.48-amd64, built with gcc 14.2.0
  Build     #1 SMP PREEMPT_DYNAMIC Debian 6.12.48-1 (2025-09-20)
  Work      in the 3 days, 4 h since boot: 912.3 million context switches (3,330
            per second), 1.2 million processes started, 51.2 million interrupts
  Now       3 tasks running or ready, 1 waiting on I/O
  Disk FS   ext4, vfat, btrfs

Pitfalls
  - A network filesystem whose server vanishes can freeze every program touching
    it, in state D (16.1): hard mounts wait forever.
  - "Stale file handle" (NFS): the file was replaced or deleted on the server
    while you held it. Reopen it, or remount.
  - FUSE is slower than a kernel filesystem: each operation goes to a program
    and back.
  - File locking and inotify watches don't work reliably on every network
    filesystem.

Deep dive

  ▾ What does the VFS keep in common?

    The objects every filesystem must provide: superblocks (a mounted
    filesystem), inodes (files), dentries (names in folders) and open file
    structures, each with a table of operations the filesystem fills in. The VFS
    handles path lookup, permissions, the page cache and caching of names and
    inodes once, for all of them.

  ▾ How do VMs and containers share folders with the host?

    virtiofs and 9p carry file operations between a guest and its host over
    virtio. virtiofs is newer, much faster, and can share the host's page cache.
    Docker Desktop and OrbStack mount the Mac's folders this way.

